      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "The equation $x^2 + y^2 = 10x - 6y - 34$ can be rearranged into $x^2 - 10x + y^2 + 6y = -34$. Completing the square on both sides, we get $x^2 - 10x + 25 + y^2 + 6y + 9 = -34 + 25 + 9$, or $(x - 5)^2 + (y + 3)^2 = 0$.\n\nThe only real solution to this is that $(x - 5) + (y + 3) = 0$, meaning $x + y = \\boxed{-8}$. Explain your process. To solve the given problem, we start with the equation of the circle:\n\n\\[x^2 + y^2 = 10x - 6y - 34.\\]\n\nFirst, we rearrange the equation to bring all terms to one side:\n\n\\[x^2 + y^2 - 10x + 6y = -34.\\]\n\nNext, we complete the square for both the \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) terms. For the \\(x\\) terms, \\(x^2 - 10x\\), we add and subtract \\((\\frac{-10}{2})^2 = 25\\):\n\n\\[x^2 - 10x = (x - 5)^2 - 25.\\]\n\nFor the \\(y\\) terms, \\(y^2 + 6y\\), we add and subtract \\((\\frac{6}{2})^2 = 9\\):\n\n\\[y^2 + 6y = (y + 3)^2 - 9.\\]\n\nSubstituting these completed square forms back into the equation, we get:\n\n\\[(x - 5)^2 - 25 + (y + 3)^2 - 9 = -34.\\]\n\nSimplifying the left side, we have:\n\n\\[(x - 5)^2 + (y + 3)^2 - 34 = -34.\\]\n\nAdding 34 to both sides of the equation, we obtain:\n\n\\[(x - 5)^2 + (y + 3)^2 = 0.\\]\n\nSince the sum of two squares is zero if and only if each square is zero, we have:\n\n\\[(x - 5)^2 = 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad (y + 3)^2 = 0.\\]\n\nThis implies:\n\n\\[x - 5 = 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad y + 3 = 0.\\]\n\nSo, we find:\n\n\\[x = 5 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad y = -3.\\]\n\nFinally, we calculate \\(x + y\\):\n\n\\[x + y = 5 + (-3) = 2.\\]\n\nSo the answer is:\n\n\\[\\boxed{2}.\\]\n\nThe correct choice is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).",
